  "prediction": "**Technology Workforce and Equal Employment Opportunity Commission (EEOC) Oversight**\n\nThe report examines the demographic trends in the technology workforce and the efforts of the U.S. Equal Employment Opportunity Commission (EEOC) and the Department of Labor's Office of Federal Contract Compliance Programs (OFCCP) to oversee technology companies and contractors.\n\n**Demographic Trends in the Technology Workforce**\n\nThe report finds that the technology workforce has grown significantly over the past decade, with an estimated 7.5 million workers in 2015. However, the workforce remains predominantly male, with women making up only 25% of the workforce. The report also finds that minority groups, including African Americans, Hispanics, and Asians, are underrepresented in the technology workforce.\n\n**EEOC and OFCCP Oversight**\n\nThe report evaluates the efforts of the EEOC and OFCCP to oversee technology companies and contractors. The EEOC is responsible for enforcing federal laws that prohibit employment discrimination, while the OFCCP is responsible for ensuring that federal contractors comply with equal employment opportunity laws.\n\nThe report finds that the EEOC and OFCCP have made efforts to increase oversight of technology companies and contractors, including conducting compliance evaluations and filing lawsuits against companies that have violated equal employment opportunity laws. However, the report also finds that there are challenges to effective oversight, including the complexity of the technology industry and the limited resources available to the EEOC and OFCCP.\n\n**Recommendations**\n\nThe report makes several recommendations to improve EEOC and OFCCP oversight of technology companies and contractors, including:\n\n1. The EEOC should develop a timeline to complete the planned effort to clean IMS data for a one-year period and add missing industry code data.\n2. The OFCCP should analyze data to identify trends and patterns in compliance evaluations and use this information to inform its oversight efforts.\n3. The OFCCP should disaggregate data on contractors by industry and company size to better understand the scope of equal employment opportunity issues in the technology industry.\n4. The OFCCP should provide more detailed guidance to contractors on their equal employment opportunity obligations and provide more frequent and transparent updates on its oversight efforts.\n\nOverall, the report highlights the importance of effective oversight of the technology industry to ensure that equal employment opportunity laws are enforced and that the workforce is diverse and inclusive.",
